Ed301-EdDSA and X301 for Fedora 45
================================

EXPERIMENTAL BUILD CANDIDATE. Not standardized, not FIPS validated.
RPM build success is not code-generation acceptance or production approval.

openssl-provider-ed301 contains four modules:
  ed301_eddsa_v2.so, ed301_eddsa_v2_tls.so,
  x301_v2.so, x301_v2_tls.so.
It does not activate any module automatically.

openssl-provider-ed301-policy requires the exact matching software package
and installs the shared OpenSSL provider configuration. Installing it makes
the two TLS-capable modules available to newly initialized, configuration-
loading OpenSSL contexts. The ordinary modules remain explicit-load-only.
The ed301 meta-package requires both matching packages.

Removing the policy package removes its owned activation file; already
running processes are not reconfigured or restarted by RPM scriptlets.
Removing the meta-package alone is not a promise that dependencies are
removed. Administrators who want to disable activation must remove the
policy package and account for existing processes and local configuration.

Fedora's selected crypto-policy and its generated backend are not replaced
or relaxed. OpenSSL's DEFAULT TLS group list remains unchanged. Applications
select the project group X301MLKEM1024 explicitly when they require it.
Hybrid-only operation must omit raw X301 from the enabled group list.

Do not load historical v1 and current v2 generations into one library
context. Old names, OIDs, raw seeds and trust assets do not constitute a
compatible v2 profile. The old four-component review meta-package is not
silently replaced: its exact OpenSSL/G301/v1 dependency set is a different
integration. Migration and rollback must be separately tested before any
workstation transition.

The Fedora build uses the repository's O3/ThinLTO/CGU1/unwind/overflow-on
profile plus the recorded distribution hardening flags. Native C flags are
accepted only by the explicit package-build opt-in patch. The ordinary
source retains its strict rejection of unapproved environment overrides.
Source, dependency lockfiles and vendor contents remain bound to the source
snapshot. No source download is performed by Cargo during the RPM build.

The build tests cover core vectors and selected existing provider/TLS/CLI
contracts. They do not replace a fresh code-generation/side-channel review
of the actual Fedora compiler outputs. LLVM-21 approval and the external
reviewer's artifact-specific LLVM-22 exception cannot be transferred to
these RPMs without their own acceptance evidence.
